{"subjectId":"sql","id":"where-operators","title":"Filtering with WHERE","group":"Querying Basics","order":12,"html":"<p class=\"lead\"><code>WHERE</code> keeps only the rows for which a condition is <strong>true</strong> — rows where it is false <em>or unknown</em> (<code>NULL</code>) are dropped.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-math-symbols\"></i>Operators</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Kind</th><th>Operators</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Comparison</td><td><code>= &lt;&gt; != &lt; &lt;= &gt; &gt;=</code></td><td><code>salary &gt;= 100000</code></td></tr><tr><td>Logical</td><td><code>AND</code>, <code>OR</code>, <code>NOT</code></td><td><code>dept_id = 1 AND salary &gt; 100000</code></td></tr><tr><td>Range</td><td><code>BETWEEN a AND b</code> (inclusive)</td><td><code>hire_date BETWEEN '2018-01-01' AND '2020-12-31'</code></td></tr><tr><td>List</td><td><code>IN (…)</code>, <code>NOT IN (…)</code></td><td><code>dept_id IN (2, 3)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Pattern</td><td><code>LIKE</code>, <code>NOT LIKE</code></td><td><code>name LIKE 'A%'</code></td></tr><tr><td>Null test</td><td><code>IS NULL</code>, <code>IS NOT NULL</code></td><td><code>email IS NULL</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-adjustments\"></i>Comparison and logic</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT name, dept_id, salary\nFROM employees\nWHERE dept_id = 1 AND salary &lt; 100000\nORDER BY emp_id;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>name  | dept_id | salary\n------+---------+-------\nMeera | 1       | 95000\nKaran | 1       | 95000</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT name, hire_date\nFROM employees\nWHERE hire_date BETWEEN '2018-01-01' AND '2020-12-31'\nORDER BY hire_date;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>name   | hire_date\n-------+-----------\nVikram | 2018-09-05\nMeera  | 2019-01-10\nKaran  | 2020-08-01</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT name, dept_id\nFROM employees\nWHERE dept_id IN (2, 3) AND NOT name = 'Sara'\nORDER BY emp_id;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>name   | dept_id\n-------+--------\nPriya  | 2\nArjun  | 2\nNeha   | 2\nVikram | 3</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-brackets\"></i>Operator precedence: AND before OR</h3>\n<p><code>AND</code> binds tighter than <code>OR</code>, exactly like <code>×</code> before <code>+</code>. Mixing them without parentheses is a classic bug.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>-- intended: Sales or HR employees earning over 75000\nSELECT name, dept_id, salary FROM employees\nWHERE dept_id = 2 OR dept_id = 3 AND salary &gt; 75000     -- means 2 OR (3 AND &gt; 75000)\nORDER BY emp_id;\n\nSELECT name, dept_id, salary FROM employees\nWHERE (dept_id = 2 OR dept_id = 3) AND salary &gt; 75000\nORDER BY emp_id;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>name   | dept_id | salary\n-------+---------+-------\nPriya  | 2       | 110000\nArjun  | 2       | 70000\nNeha   | 2       | 72000\nVikram | 3       | 80000\nSara   | 3       | 85000\n\nname   | dept_id | salary\n-------+---------+-------\nPriya  | 2       | 110000\nVikram | 3       | 80000\nSara   | 3       | 85000</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>The first query returned Arjun and Neha (Sales, below 75000) because the salary test only applied to HR. When <code>AND</code> and <code>OR</code> meet, always add parentheses.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-calendar\"></i>BETWEEN with dates and times</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p><code>BETWEEN '2024-03-01' AND '2024-03-31'</code> misses <code>'2024-03-31 14:00'</code> when the column stores timestamps — the string/timestamp is greater than the upper bound. Prefer a half-open range: <code>ts &gt;= '2024-03-01' AND ts &lt; '2024-04-01'</code>.</p></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT order_id, order_date FROM orders\nWHERE order_date &gt;= '2024-02-01' AND order_date &lt; '2024-03-01'\nORDER BY order_id;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>order_id | order_date\n---------+-----------\n103      | 2024-02-03\n104      | 2024-02-14\n105      | 2024-02-20</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is the difference between != and &lt;&gt;?</summary><div><p>None in practice — <code>&lt;&gt;</code> is the ISO standard “not equal”, <code>!=</code> is accepted by every major engine. Both yield unknown (not true) when either side is <code>NULL</code>.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Is BETWEEN inclusive?</summary><div><p>Yes, both ends: <code>x BETWEEN a AND b</code> means <code>x &gt;= a AND x &lt;= b</code>. If <code>a &gt; b</code> it matches nothing (SQL Server/MySQL) — <code>BETWEEN SYMMETRIC</code> in PostgreSQL swaps them for you.</p></div></details></section>"}